OpenHamShack is the userspace for an Intel N150 mini PC that replaces Portsdown and the Lynx player.

What it does
- Intel Quick Sync (VA-API, iHD driver) encodes H.264 or H.265.
- ffmpeg packs a CBR MPEG-TS a few percent under the DVB net rate.
- UDP port 8282 is the ingest used by F5OEO Pluto DATV firmware and by the LibreSDR DVBS2 firmware (DL1JM / PA2JSA). H.265 rides this port.
- RTMP port 7272 is the H.264-only alternative. Settings are stuffed in the URL.
- Optional MQTT to plutodvb/subvar on the Pluto (port 1883). LibreSDR leaves this off; set the frequency on the radio page.
- MiniTiouner or PicoTuner (G4EML USB firmware) plugs in over USB. longmynd demodulates, patched with longmynd-picotuner.patch to open the PicoTuner too.
- PicoTuner uses WinterHill ethernet firmware. Tune commands go to UDP 9920. The transport stream returns on UDP 9941.

Write the disk
- Boot a Debian or Ubuntu machine as root, with the network up.
- ./mkimage.sh
- Write openhamshack.img to the mini PC's disk or a USB stick with any image writer, and boot UEFI.
- No password login: both accounts are locked and SSH takes only the key built in with SSH_KEYS.
- Open port 8080 on the mini PC. The radio must already be running DVB firmware.

The picture is not on the air until you press the button. Bare Pluto and LibreSDR output is about 0 dBm. Use a filter and a linear PA, on a frequency your licence allows.
